title: LSP Editor Setup

!! Connecting an editor

!!! Editors that attach to a socket

Neovim connects to a server that is already running, which is the mode that shares a process with `--mcp`. Register the filetype first, since `.tid` is not one Neovim knows:

```
vim.filetype.add({ extension = { tid = "tid" } })

vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("FileType", {
	pattern = "tid",
	callback = function()
		vim.lsp.start({
			name = "tiddlywiki",
			cmd = vim.lsp.rpc.connect("127.0.0.1", 6009),
			root_dir = vim.fn.getcwd(),
		})
	end,
})
```

!!! Editors that launch a command

Helix and most others expect to start the server themselves and talk to it over stdin and stdout. That needs its own TiddlyWiki process, because `--mcp` cannot share stdio (see [[LSP and MCP]]):

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --lsp stdio
```

!!! VS Code

The [[vscode-tiddlywiki-lsp|https://github.com/wikilabs/vscode-tiddlywiki-lsp]] extension connects VS Code to a running `--lsp` server; its README says how to install it. It binds to the `tid` and `tiddlywiki5` languages the tw5-syntax extension registers, and dials port 6009 unless its settings say otherwise.

A shadow tiddler, or a tiddler without a file of its own, opens read-only under the extension's `tiddlywiki:` scheme when you go to a definition in it or pick it from a list. After updating the extension, reload the VS Code window so the scheme is registered.

!! Saving

The running wiki does not watch its folder, so the server reads a `.tid` you save in the editor into the wiki itself: hovers, globals and references then answer from the saved text, with no `reload_tiddlers` and no restart. Only a file the wiki already files a tiddler in, or a new `.tid` in its tiddlers folder, is read, so a plugin's source file never becomes a tiddler of its own. When a save changes a tiddler's title, the new title takes the file and the old one leaves the wiki. The server log names each tiddler it reloads.

The editor has to tell the server about saves; VS Code does. Try it in [[LSP Saving - (Example)]].
